From 26ffe7e99a1e75ddf42c436a209621eb0fe9ee5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:13:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep one clock between two players, and make a flag a message

The receiving side never charged the clock: receivePeerAction applied the
move and left the clock alone, so each player's own clock paid for both
sides' thinking between their own moves, and the opponent's clock on screen
jumped back up on every move. Each side also decided a flag alone and never
said so.

- A move carries the mover's account of both clocks; the receiver charges
  the mover for the elapsed time, then adopts the mover's own number for the
  mover's side, capped at its own view plus SYNC_TOLERANCE (3s). No report,
  or a malformed one, leaves the local view alone, so an older build still
  plays.
- A flag is sent as control { kind: 'flag', side }. Your own is called at
  once; the opponent's is called locally only after the tolerance, as the
  fallback. A claim that we flagged is checked against our own clock.
- A move arriving after the match ended is ignored, not applied over it.
- Both king placements are timed, on both screens.

test/clock.test.js replays the protocol over eighty plies at 250ms latency
and asserts the two views never part by more than the tolerance. Verified in
Chromium for the bot path: placement charged, play ticking, flag copy. Not
verified with two real peers. CACHE v42 -> v43.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
